# /etc/wg-failover/<iface>.peers   — one file per tunnel: wg0 … wg4
#
# Rename to drop .example to activate. The interface is taken from the
# filename, so it is not a field.
#
# FORMAT — pipe-delimited, six fields, no quoting, no spaces around the pipes:
#
#   public_key|endpoint_ip|endpoint_port|label|load|flags
#
#   1 public_key    base64, 44 chars ending '='. Per SERVER, not per key —
#                   every Proton server has its own, so a candidate is always
#                   a (key, server) pair. From logicals.json:
#                   .LogicalServers[].Servers[].X25519PublicKey
#
#   2 endpoint_ip   LITERAL IP, never a hostname. This is the one that matters:
#                   DNS is hijacked to the resolver, whose own upstream goes
#                   through a tunnel — so at the exact moment failover runs,
#                   name resolution may be the thing that is broken. An IP has
#                   no such dependency. From .Servers[].EntryIP
#
#   3 endpoint_port 443. UDP 51820 is filtered by Digi/RCS-RDS.
#
#   4 label         free text for logread, e.g. 'CH#12 SecureCore CH-AT'.
#                   No pipes. Keep it short; it is what you read at 3am.
#
#   5 load          server load 0-100 at GENERATION time, or empty. Used only
#                   to break ties between candidates within 5 ms of each other
#                   — it is a snapshot and goes stale within the hour, so it is
#                   never the primary sort. From .LogicalServers[].Load
#
#   6 flags         comma-separated, or empty. Recognised: p2p, securecore,
#                   tor, streaming. Derived from .LogicalServers[].Features
#                   (bitmask: 1=securecore 2=tor 4=p2p 8=streaming 16=ipv6).
#
#                   p2p is ENFORCED, not decorative: a .peers file whose name
#                   is listed in REQUIRE_P2P (see the script) will skip any
#                   candidate without it. Proton gates P2P by server role, not
#                   by key attribute — a NetShield-off key on a non-P2P server
#                   still will not carry torrent traffic, which is why your
#                   earlier attempt failed.
#
# Ordering in the file does not matter. The script re-ranks by live ICMP RTT
# hourly and caches to /var/run/wg-failover/<iface>.ranked. Force a re-rank:
#   rm /var/run/wg-failover/*.ranked.at
#
# Five to ten candidates per tunnel is the right number. Fewer and a regional
# outage exhausts the list; more and the hourly probe gets slow and noisy.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Example — PRIVATE via Secure Core:
#
# yAnB2q...uK4=|185.159.157.10|443|CH#5 SC CH-AT|32|securecore
# 7Kd0Le...Pw8=|185.159.157.11|443|CH#7 SC CH-AT|41|securecore
# Qm3xTv...Nc2=|193.148.16.20|443|IS#2 SC IS-CH|28|securecore
#
# Example — MEDIA, where p2p is required:
#
# Lp9zRt...Hb6=|149.88.24.30|443|RO#3 P2P|55|p2p
# Vn4kYw...Ds1=|149.88.24.31|443|RO#5 P2P|38|p2p
